    "title": "ParetoQ: Improving Scaling Laws in Extremely Low-bit LLM Quantization",
    "citation": "Ze-Chun Liu et al., 2025",
    "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.02631",
    "authors": "Ze-Chun Liu; Changsheng Zhao; Hanxian Huang; Si-Jia Chen; Jing Zhang; Jia-Wei Zhao; Scott Roy; Lisa Jin; Yun-Yang Xiong; Yang-Yang Shi; Lin Xiao; Yuandong Tian; Bilge Soran; Raghuraman Krishnamoorthi; Tijmen Blankevoort; Vikas Chandra",
    "year": "2025",
    "original_source": false
  },
  "page_placements": [],
  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "Tests the scale-related downstream-performance part of V6 by reporting downstream benchmark accuracy across parameter sizes, but uses quantized-model accuracy rather than held-out pretraining loss or a fitted scaling law.",
    "gate_note": "The paper studies quantization accuracy, training-token allocation, and accuracy/size or accuracy/speed trade-offs. It does not report held-out pretraining cross-entropy power-law fits, extrapolation tests, or compute-optimal parameter/token allocations. Only the reported downstream average-accuracy size sweeps are recorded for V6. Other quantization results and cited prior-work results are not rows for the listed versions.",
    "test_reason": "A quantization-method paper. Its 'scaling laws' are accuracy-versus-effective-quantized-size Pareto curves across bit-widths. It reports its own method's accuracy on off-the-shelf pretrained models (MobileLLM, LLaMA-3) that were trained on different data and recipes. It does not fit or test loss power laws, extrapolation or compute-optimal allocation. Its size sweeps are not a controlled test of downstream predictability with scale: it fits no trend, mixes model families and does not relate scores to pretraining loss. The recorded rows compare ParetoQ with full precision at the same size, which measures the quantization gap, not a scale effect.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly correct. The authors also argue that training scheme and quantization function must be added to low-bit scaling-law analysis, L(N,D,P,S_train,F). They further argue that a 2-bit kernel offers better accuracy-speed trade-offs on CPU, though not for small models on GPU.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study evaluates quantized-model downstream accuracy and accuracy/size trade-offs, not held-out pretraining cross-entropy scaling or compute-optimal allocation. It varies quantization bit-width and QAT settings.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The paper reports outcomes varying with quantization bit-width, quantization function, training-token allocation, model size, and kernel/hardware setup.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "After QAT with ParetoQ, 1.58-, 2- and 3-bit models match or beat 4-bit at equal effective quantized model size on MobileLLM and LLaMA-3 (Fig. 7, 12–14).",
        "proposed_claim": "Precision-scaling: at fixed memory, sub-4-bit QAT models lie on the accuracy-size Pareto frontier.",
        "row_ids": []
      },
      {
        "finding": "The choice of quantization function and training scheme changes low-bit 'scaling law' conclusions (Finding-3, Fig. 6).",
        "proposed_claim": "Low-precision scaling-law conclusions depend on the quantizer and training scheme.",
        "row_ids": []
      },
      {
        "finding": "With a 100B-token budget on MobileLLM-125M, accuracy peaks with about 90% FP pretraining and 10% QAT (Fig. 2).",
        "proposed_claim": "Optimal token split between full-precision training and QAT.",
        "row_ids": []
      }
    ],
    "controls": "n",
    "capability_check": "y",
    "uncertainty_reported": "n",
    "code_released": "not stated",
    "peer_reviewed": "Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2025)",
    "problems": "The downstream scaling evidence is benchmark accuracy rather than held-out pretraining loss; no predictive curve or uncertainty estimates are reported. The reported model-size sweeps use different model families and quantization settings, so they do not establish the original loss-scaling laws.",
    "rows_left_out": "No rows left out. Other tables report separately many benchmark metrics and quantization methods; they were not included because the recorded V6 rows use the paper's reported average across eight zero-shot tasks for ParetoQ runs.",
    "untested": [
      {
